y how they got there it isn't  until the third to last episode of the  season vaulting ambition that we learn  gabriel lorca is a mirror universe  native having switched places with his  prime counterpart before the events of  the series lorca sees the potential of  discovery's spore drive to bring him  back home so he can continue his  rebellion against emperor philippa  giorgio  just the fact that lurka is able to pull  the wool over so many viewers eyes is  enough to secure him a spot on this list  but isaac's superb acting and the  layered conception of the character  earns the accolades all on their own  lorca is able to fool us precisely  because he isn't simply a mustache  twirling bad guy while he's using the  discovery crew for his own ends he bears  no ill will toward them and in some ways  the respect and care he shows for them  is genuine until it isn't i couldn't  have imagined but lorca was an impostor  from an alternate universe was not the  most obvious conclusion  we were all deceived  a lot of fans would balk at the idea of  having the andorian commander shrand  being labeled a villain portrayed by  jeffrey combs shran who is fiercely  loyal to his people was introduced in  star trek enterprise's first season we  meet shran in an episode titled the  andorian incident where he leads a squad  of his blue skinned brethren in the  violent takeover of a vulcan monastery  shrand and the other andorians come off  as nothing more than paranoid and  xenophobic bullies but despite their  thuggish behavior captain archer  discovers they're right about the  monastery being a cover for a vulcan  facility that monitors the andorians  when archer exposes the truth he earns  an uneasy ally in sran shran comes to  the aid of archer and the enterprise on  more than one occasion in season three  shran bails the heroes out twice  including the final battle to save earth  from the zindi's doomsday weapon but  there's always volatility working under  shiran's surface threatening to once  more put him at odds with archer and his  crew it happens for example in season  4's united when trans stubborn refusal  to give the telerites the benefit of the  doubt leads to archer challenging him to  a death duel  for the most part christopher lloyd's  most famous characters like doc brown in  the back to the future series aren't  particularly intimidating so it's a  testament to the actor's range that he  performed so well as the klingon  commander krug in 1984's star trek iii  the search for spock when krug learns of  starfleet's secret genesis project meant  to instantly turn lifeless celestial  bodies into habitable planets he means  to seize it as a doomsday weapon for the  klingon empire in the process he orders  the killing of captain kirk's son a  death that scars kirk for years in many  ways krug is the first example of the  kind of klingon we know and love today  while their new look is shown briefly in  1979 star trek the motion picture it  isn't until krug that we get to see a  fully fleshed out performance while the  klingons were always warlike krug and  his crew are more brutish than their  predecessors in the original series  locking himself in a death grip with an  alien beast just for fun and going mono  amano with kirk as genesis is in its  explosive death throes krug gives us a  portrayal of what remains one of the  most potent examples of the klingon  passion for combat  you don't find a lot of trek villains  wearing starfleet uniforms after all the  desire to join starfleet is usually  accompanied by a drive to uphold the  values of the united federation of  planets but one notable exception is  lieutenant commander michael eddington  played by kenneth marshall introduced in  ds9 season 3 premiere eddington starts  out as an officer who appears to be  overly concerned with promotion and more  than willing to turn in the heroes of  ds9 if they dare disobey their starfleet  superiors toward the end of season 4 we  learn eddington's seemingly tireless  devotion to starfleet is a ruse in the  episode for the cause eddington tricks  most of ds9's command crew away from the  station and in their absence steals a  shipment of industrial replicators he  then reveals himself as a member of the  maquis a group of violent rebels who  oppose the federation's treaty with  cardassia  the very fact that eddington is able to  trick benjamin cisco played by avery  brooks makes the starfleet's commander  turned captain obsessed with capturing  the traitor he's one of the few villains  capable of getting so thoroughly under a  captain's skin to the point where said  captain actually humors some rather  questionable tactics and apprehension  and if it takes me the rest of my life i  will see you standing before a court  martial that'll break you  the flamboyant and deceptive harry mudd  is one of the few named antagonists from  the original series to enjoy a second  appearance played by roger c carmel he  first shows up in season one's muds  women trying to sell women like cattle  and using an illegal drug to render them  physically irresistible later in season  two's i mud the con man gives up the  enterprise screw to a group of androids  with dreams of galactic conquest carmel  returned to voice his famous crook in an  episode of star trek the animated series  called mud's passion in the episode he  runs foul of the enterprise once more  after attempting to con the inhabitants  of an alien planet into believing he  could sell them starfleet academy that  wouldn't be the end of mud rayne wilson  plays a more vicious version of harry  mudd in star trek discovery's first  season captain lorca meets mud in the  episode choose your pain where they're  both prisoners of the klingons lorca and  lieutenant ash tyler leave mud behind  after they escape the klingons and the  con man gets revenge with a plot  involving a time manipulation device in  the episode magic to make the sanest man  go mad  one of the most popular pieces of trek  mythology introduced in ds9 is the  clandestine organization section 31 and  william sadler's brilliant performance  as luther sloan the director of section  31 during the events of ds9 makes him  one of the most memorable villains  suspecting dr bashir played by alexander  siddig of being an unwitting spy for the  dominion sloan first shows up on ds9  using a holodeck to find out the truth  he subjects bashir to an elaborate  illusion in which all of his friends on  ds9 turn their backs on him once beshear  discovers the truth sloan is convinced  of his innocence and offers him a  position with section 31 despite  beshear's clear refusal sloan returns to  use the doctor in a covert operation  involving the infiltration of the  romulan empire toward the end of the  series we learned sloan is behind the  poisoning of odo and all of his fellow  changelings with a deadly plague sloan  is ruthless cunning and mysterious but  he's surprisingly sympathetic he's aware  of the hypocrisy of betraying the  federation's core values in order to  protect it yet he still believes in what  he's doing and has no ill will towards  bashir for despising him he sees bashir  as exactly the kind of man he was born  to protect even if safeguarding him  earns him nothing but bashir's disgust  introduced in the original series the  mirror universe is an alternate  dimension where the federation is  replaced by the tyrannical terran empire  rather than living by values like  tolerance diplomacy and discovery the  terence are a brutal people who believe  in conquest and cruelty in the ds9  episode crossover dr bashir and major  kira played by nana visitor find  themselves in the alternate universe  where they learn that since the events  of the original series the terran empire  has been conquered by the klingon  cardassian alliance terence have become  a slave race and ds9 retaining its  original cardassian name tariq noor is  led by intended kira intended kira is  the cold-blooded counterpart to major  kira and the character gives visitor a  chance to show us her acting range aside  from sharing natural leadership  qualities intended kira is a wild  departure from major kira she's  seductive with an unapologetically  endless appetite for pleasure using  cisco a pirate in the mirror universe as  her personal plaything she's as vicious  as any of the kardashians or klingons  under her command and deceptively  sadistic sometimes acting as if she's  about to absolve her victims before  ordering their executions intended kira  was far too much fun for just a single  ds9 episode so she became a recurring  villain  you hear a lot about villains you love  to hate but then there are villains you  just straight up hate kai win is  definitely one of the latter luis  fletcher plays win on ds9 beginning as  an ambitious medic of a rigid religious  order on bajor wynn is a slippery and  manipulative vedic working behind the  scenes in coup and assassination  attempts her actions she claims are  always at the service of the wormhole  dwelling aliens which majorans worship  and call the prophets yet it seems she's  always working towards her own ascension  in the ranks of bajor's clergy she  succeeds in becoming bajor's spiritual  leader the kai but by the end of the  series her jealousy towards ben cisco  her seduction by goldikott and her anger  towards the prophets lead her to abandon  her religion and follow the devilish  parades  wynn is one of the most interesting and  complex villains in all of trek her  downfall is tragic in the most classic  sense of the word once she wins the  title of kai rather than simply basking  in her power as we expect she seems to  genuinely want to help her people the  problem is of course she also seems to  fully believe that the ends justify any  and all means what if you could  genetically engineer a race to be  equally cutting leaders and diplomats in  the most hostile of situations what you  would come up with might look a lot like  the vorta which is the dominion's  lieutenant race that leads hordes of  brutal gemhadar while being completely  subservient to the founders and of the  vorta we meet on ds9 none is more  memorable than jeffrey combs weiyou  while weiyoun dies at the end of his  first appearance in season 4's to the  death the villain was just too good to  discard entirely so it was decided that  the founders keep clones of the vorta  constantly at the ready complete with  the memories of their predecessors as  the head of the dominion's military  presence in the alpha quadrant weion  frequently shows up in the last two  seasons of ds9 and he steals every scene  he's in he's dishonest and manipulative  without shame ready to shift from  intimidating to conciliatory at a  moment's notice in scenes with  politically influential ds9 characters  like ben cisco and gul dukat wein uses  conversation like a deadly evasive  weapon always adapting to his  counterparts mood with everything from  wrath to abject apology he lies so  easily that you wonder whether or not  weiyu knows or even cares what's true or  false  any list of best star trek villains that  doesn't include ricardo montalban's khan  needs to delete everything and start  over first appearing in the original  series episode space seed khan is a  genetically enhanced despot who ruled  over a quarter of the earth before  escaping the eugenic wars with 84 of his  followers the enterprise crew regrets  waking khan and his people from  suspended animation once they take over  the ship while khan is a fun villain in  his first appearance his appeal doesn't  reach full bloom until 1982's star trek  ii the wrath of khan which is arguably  the best star trek movie blaming kirk  for the tragedies that have befallen him  since they crossed paths years earlier  khan and his followers take over the uss  reliant and wage war on the enterprise  while khan's followers believe they're  using the stolen federation property for  power and prosperity it's soon clear  their leader is willing to sacrifice  everything including his own life and  theirs to get revenge on kirk i mean to  avenge myself upon you admiral  i've deprived your shape of power and  when i swing around i mean to deprive  you of your life  through montalban khan becomes the  absolute embodiment of all consuming  vengeance the villain regularly either  paraphrases or directly quotes lines  from herman melville's novel moby dick  and just as captain ahab is obsessed  with destroying the titular whale khan  ultimately has no purpose left but  vengeance against kirk  in tng's cue who episode the borg one of  the federation's deadliest enemies are  introduced the cybernetic zombie-like  hive-minded baddies kill with impunity  however their goal is not just to kill  but to assimilate the technology and  biology of other intelligent species  into their collective in a quest for  ultimate perfection there are no  individuals within the borg collective  but in 1996's star trek first contact  were introduced to the borg queen the  living embodiment of the collective's  will the borg queen does something you  wouldn't think possible with the borg  she makes them sexy in fact she spends  most of her scenes in first contact  seducing data with promises of pleasures  he's never been capable of fully  experiencing but she's far from just a  temptress particularly in her subsequent  appearances in star trek voyager the  borah queen played by both alice krieg  and suzanne thompson at different points  conveys the singular horror of being  confronted by a being who is in her own  words one who is many speaking to looper  in early 2020 krieg shared interesting  insights about playing the cybernetic  villain including how the character came  to be krieg explained i think she was an  entity that happened at the moment of  creation and she's always been that and  she always will be and it's entirely  abnormal  perhaps no other single villain has  appeared in as many star trek series is  john de lancie's all-powerful q showing  up for the premiere of tng its finale  and a bunch of fun episodes in between q  is a mischievous god-like being who  defies definition he would eventually  come to harass voyager's captain janeway  almost as much as he did picard along  with brief but memorable appearances on  ds9 and star trek lower decks hilarious  and carefree q is so popular that it  might be a problem for some fans to even  call him a villain but while he isn't  wholly evil he's certainly a villain in  the sense that when he shows up he's  almost always an antagonist while he  usually reverses any direct harm he  inflicts upon humans he couldn't care  less about those who are lost indirectly  such as the enterprise crew members  killed by the borg in the episode cue  who whether you see him as a good guy or  a bad guy delancey's portrayal of the  trickster is one of the most delightful  parts of tng and voyager after all  without him we'd never see a mariachi  band on the enterprise bridge we'd never  see gainen plunge a fork into a dude's  hand and most importantly we'd never see  the enterprise crew in sherwood forest  get out of my chair  and i was hoping for something more  along the lines of welcome back hugh  it's a pleasure to see you again my old  friend  one of the aspects where ds9 excels over  other trek series is that its serialized  storytelling allows its characters to  evolve and you can see this evolution  clearly in the cardassian tyrant  golducot formerly the prefect of bajor  during its occupation ducat is none too  happy to find a human sitting in his old  office in ds9's premiere episode this  fixation on cisco and his crushing sense  of inadequacy grow over the course of  the series to the point where it  suffocates him the ducats we meet in  ds9's premiere isn't the ducat we see in  its finale a classic cardassian ducats  spends the first few seasons of ds9  working towards the goal of once again  ruling over the people of bajor he's not  only unwilling to face the atrocities he  committed but he's so delusional that he  resents the bajorans for refusing to  honor him with statues and plaques  for a time he's something of an ally to  the ds9 crew but when the dominion gives  him an opportunity to seize control over  all of cardassia he becomes the alpha  quadrant's most feared warlord before  the end of the series he goes from  conqueror to madman madman to cult  leader and from cult leader to the  powerful vessel of the paw wraiths the  prophet's destructive adversaries  wonderfully portrayed by mark alimo  ducat is ultimately the most complex  believable and twisted villain in all of  star trek  check out one of our newest videos right  here plus even more looper videos about  star trek are coming soon subscribe to  our youtube channel and hit the bell so  you don't miss a single one  [Music]
 

